WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.390
Especially in this day and age with AI, how could you not recognize that privacy on Bitcoin

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:10.099
right now is a huge issue? I think there's also quantum, which to me is just shocking,

00:00:10.099 --> 00:00:14.117
especially now with the US administration that accelerated the deadline to,

00:00:14.117 --> 00:00:17.240
I think it's 2031 now, from 2035 previously.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.640
And knowing that Bitcoin will take years to upgrade to post-quantum,

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:25.640
once there is consensus on it, it feels irresponsible, frankly.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:29.735
If you're optimizing for usable money that protects people's security and safety and

00:00:29.735 --> 00:00:33.380
all those things, then you have to optimize for something else.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.411
But this should be the goal, is to optimize for this thing and not optimize for just

00:00:37.411 --> 00:00:38.580
the price going up.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:40.060
Hi, Josh Swihart.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.600
Hey, how are you?

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.867
Starting. Yeah, no, it's been great. First question, like sort of like starting with

00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:52.698
the history of Zcash. I mean, you've been involved since quite a while now in Zcash and

00:00:52.698 --> 00:00:59.014
helping building it. Did you ever think that at some point, before sort of like the Zcash

00:00:59.014 --> 00:01:01.660
comeback, Zcash was sort of dying?

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:07.010
It was objectively dying. So that was like in 2023, like I resigned from ECC,

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:13.487
and I resigned from what I thought would be my life's work, like what would be where

00:01:13.487 --> 00:01:19.560
I ended my career, what I would do for the rest of my life.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.864
I didn't resign from Zcash. I resigned from ECC, and in part because I felt like there

00:01:24.864 --> 00:01:31.204
was change that needed to be made in order for the project to be successful.

00:01:31.204 --> 00:01:34.628
So we had languished for quite some time.

00:01:34.628 --> 00:01:41.241
The price was in the doldrums, maybe, you know, $20, and a dip below that.

00:01:41.241 --> 00:01:46.153
I think objectively that's a measure of whether or not there's success,

00:01:46.153 --> 00:01:51.616
because of the limited supply. We would joke internally that there were probably 30 people

00:01:51.616 --> 00:01:56.001
in the world that cared about the project, and we kept talking to those 30 people.

00:01:56.001 --> 00:02:00.660
Some of that was happening during the time where there was this sandblasting attack,

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.236
where people couldn't use ZEC at all. It was effectively bricked.

00:02:05.236 --> 00:02:11.769
Somebody who goes by Peacemonger was running a project called Jour and was doing user

00:02:11.769 --> 00:02:17.469
studies and doing interviews with users, and one of the things she was tracking was net

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:21.426
promoter score, like would you recommend this to a friend.

00:02:21.426 --> 00:02:25.910
And it was like as far negative as you could go.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.776
So even the people that were diehard Zcashers were hating on Zcash.

00:02:30.776 --> 00:02:31.276
Yeah.

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:36.500
And people wanted to use it, and they couldn't use it, and they were frustrated,

00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:40.740
and they were frustrated with where things were going.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.092
And so I had resigned before Zcon. I don't remember which number that was,

00:02:45.092 --> 00:02:50.403
but that was in Barcelona. And so Barcelona, that was like my last day.

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:56.220
And I had started writing, and some others had started writing on the forums about what

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:01.956
needed to change. Somebody else had made a post about it being like a grand stadium that

00:03:01.956 --> 00:03:04.916
had been built, and nobody's showing up to the stadium.

00:03:04.916 --> 00:03:05.416
Yeah.

00:03:05.416 --> 00:03:08.436
And I got to Zcon, and the whole thing was dark.

00:03:08.436 --> 00:03:12.235
Like the rooms were all dark. There were just a few people there,

00:03:12.235 --> 00:03:16.361
compared to like the first Zcon, which is in Montreal, it's Zcon0,

00:03:16.361 --> 00:03:20.029
where it was just vibrant and alive, and all of these people were there,

00:03:20.029 --> 00:03:24.447
and not just Zcash people, but people that were across the crypto ecosystem and kind

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:28.567
of were interested in this new novel thing that had been built.

00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:33.178
And I left that conference thinking, man, this was like the right decision to leave,

00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:34.452
because it is...

00:03:34.452 --> 00:03:38.320
Yeah, it felt so dead. What do you think revived it?

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:43.593
Was it the price action? I mean, I have my take.

00:03:43.593 --> 00:03:48.856
I think it's the Zashi effect. But was it solving the UX?

00:03:48.856 --> 00:03:51.216
Is it like a combination of those things?

00:03:51.216 --> 00:03:53.366
Like what actually led to the comeback?

00:03:53.366 --> 00:03:55.889
Yeah, I think it was a convergence of things.

00:03:55.889 --> 00:04:00.887
So first, it wasn't usable, and people didn't have a wallet that they actually liked

00:04:00.887 --> 00:04:07.551
or they trusted or they wanted to use. And for a long time, ECC strategy was very confused.

00:04:07.551 --> 00:04:15.540
So like when I even first interviewed, I had mentioned that ECC needed a wallet,

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:21.260
and that was back in, I think I first interviewed in '17, 2017,

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:26.103
to come on board. And the response was, nah, somebody else will build it,

00:04:26.103 --> 00:04:28.009
we're just gonna focus on the protocol.

00:04:28.009 --> 00:04:28.509
Yeah.

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:30.730
It's like, but the user experience, this is terrible, right?

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.999
In order to send a shielded transaction, you know, at the time it's still command line,

00:04:34.999 --> 00:04:39.891
and it just wasn't usable. It wasn't going to get adoption.

00:04:39.891 --> 00:04:40.391
Yeah.

00:04:40.391 --> 00:04:44.021
And so it's like, well, we don't want to do it, we don't want to have a crowding out,

00:04:44.021 --> 00:04:45.952
so we're going to build the SDKs.

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.920
Yeah, and I think at the time, even like the shielded transaction,

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:53.862
the share of shielded transactions was like single-digit percentage numbers,

00:04:53.862 --> 00:04:57.992
right? Like it was just so hard to make a shielded transaction,

00:04:57.992 --> 00:05:01.763
due to like the cryptography required and how long it took.

00:05:01.763 --> 00:05:02.263
Yeah.

00:05:02.263 --> 00:05:04.354
It's just not the same compared to today.

00:05:04.354 --> 00:05:08.609
Yeah, you had to be like way to the left of the adoption curve.

00:05:08.609 --> 00:05:09.109
Yeah.

00:05:09.109 --> 00:05:12.239
In order to use ZEC. It was just impossible.

00:05:12.239 --> 00:05:15.758
And part of that was the nature of the cryptography.

00:05:15.758 --> 00:05:19.016
So we were still on Sprout, the initial shielded pool.

00:05:19.016 --> 00:05:23.414
It was not performant. You couldn't run it, you couldn't create a proof on a mobile device.

00:05:23.414 --> 00:05:28.293
So if you're going to use it, you had to basically, you had to use transparent transactions.

00:05:28.293 --> 00:05:30.525
Any exchanges, no exchanges could use it.

00:05:30.525 --> 00:05:31.985
It didn't scale.

00:05:31.985 --> 00:05:36.989
And so, you know, Sapling didn't arrive until the end of 2018.

00:05:36.989 --> 00:05:44.955
At that point it could be used, but it was still, ECC's strategy at the time was still

00:05:44.955 --> 00:05:51.641
to like provide SDKs and maybe sometime provide a wallet, and started working on a reference wallet.

00:05:51.641 --> 00:05:54.339
And it didn't really work, and it was way too clumsy.

00:05:54.339 --> 00:05:59.920
There were some things structurally within ECC that were broken that didn't allow us

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:06.218
to ship really well. And so then by the time we got to like the sandblasting attack,

00:06:06.218 --> 00:06:09.108
which then lasted a year, it was just god-awful.

00:06:09.108 --> 00:06:16.380
So what changed was a combination of: we needed to change the structure of ECC and how

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:20.623
engineers were working together and how we prioritized our work.

00:06:20.623 --> 00:06:24.287
We needed to create an opinionated wallet where we could say,

00:06:24.287 --> 00:06:28.332
this is best in class and this is how a wallet should be built.

00:06:28.332 --> 00:06:33.816
Get that launched, get user feedback on what is working, what is not working,

00:06:33.816 --> 00:06:37.700
use that user feedback to determine what we build into the protocol.

00:06:37.700 --> 00:06:43.002
And we started iterating on that, and as soon as we released that wallet,

00:06:43.002 --> 00:06:46.100
adoption of the shielded curve started to grow.

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.750
And then there was this other convergence of things, where the market,

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:54.804
I think, was starting to catch up. It was usable.

00:06:54.804 --> 00:06:59.343
We added hardware support with Keystone, partnership with Keystone,

00:06:59.343 --> 00:07:10.300
late 2024, and then the shielded pool started to grow exponentially at that point.

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:14.419
And then recognized that we needed actually swaps in and out.

00:07:14.419 --> 00:07:17.539
And that was the big factor. Yeah, swaps.

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:22.028
Like to me, integrating NEAR Intents into Zodl, formerly Zashi,

00:07:22.028 --> 00:07:27.572
was really such a good call. Like it's, in retrospect, such an obvious decision.

00:07:27.572 --> 00:07:33.681
I don't know if I would have expected it to work as well as it did prior to that,

00:07:33.681 --> 00:07:37.100
because, I mean, many wallets have like built-in swaps, right?

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:40.460
And it didn't strike me as such, like, oh, it's obviously going to work.

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:43.060
I think for Zcash, like the differentiating effect was privacy,

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.623
right? Like there's the swap, but you don't know where it comes from,

00:07:46.623 --> 00:07:49.664
you don't know where to, you know. Yeah, that was such a good call.

00:07:49.664 --> 00:07:53.618
Yeah, yeah. It was one of those things where we were exploring it.

00:07:53.618 --> 00:07:57.296
Some other people that are Zcashers were working, also engaged,

00:07:57.296 --> 00:08:01.093
they've engaged with the NEAR ecosystem, and they made some introductions and started

00:08:01.093 --> 00:08:03.227
meeting with the NEAR team. The NEAR team at first was like,

00:08:03.227 --> 00:08:06.340
we're not going to support privacy, it's like too scary, too risky.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:06.988
Tornado Cash.

00:08:06.988 --> 00:08:09.904
Yeah, yeah. And so everybody was afraid of it.

00:08:09.904 --> 00:08:14.697
Like we had, yeah, we had started trying to work with THORChain like a long time ago,

00:08:14.697 --> 00:08:17.720
and THORChain was just like, we're not touching it, right after Tornado Cash.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:23.240
So we started working with NEAR. NEAR's like, okay, we'll support transparent transactions,

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.415
but no way we're doing anything with shielded.

00:08:26.415 --> 00:08:30.560
And then some other people were very skeptical that it would work.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.920
Like, how can you have a single-currency wallet and allow swaps in it,

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:35.789
in and out? Like, nobody's gonna use it.

00:08:35.789 --> 00:08:36.289
Yeah.

00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:37.836
And it proved to be just the opposite.

00:08:37.836 --> 00:08:39.893
Yeah, I know, it's the contrary. It's exceptional.

00:08:39.893 --> 00:08:44.053
Yeah, that's really great. You mentioned things were like broken at ECC.

00:08:44.053 --> 00:08:49.093
What were some of those things? I think you've already expressed the fact that the funding

00:08:49.093 --> 00:08:56.102
model for ECC was sort of like broken, because, I think, you were relying on like block

00:08:56.102 --> 00:09:00.391
revenues, essentially. Was that part of what was broken?

00:09:00.391 --> 00:09:03.520
Was there something else as well?

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.707
Yeah, so that creates... So ECC at one point was a for-profit company when it launched.

00:09:08.707 --> 00:09:12.000
So it was Zcash Company, created the open source.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:18.112
Then a decision was made at ECC to move it under a not-for-profit structure.

00:09:18.112 --> 00:09:21.056
There's various views on why that was done.

00:09:21.056 --> 00:09:24.334
Like internally, there was conversations about what is optics,

00:09:24.334 --> 00:09:28.880
is that people want, if we're going to continue to receive block rewards,

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.960
people want that to go to a not-for-profit, not for a for-profit.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:35.776
I think there was a desire to clean the cap table.

00:09:35.776 --> 00:09:40.800
And so Bootstrap was created. So Bootstrap is a 501(c)(3).

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.920
And then, rather than try to convert ECC into a 501(c)(3), which would be very difficult,

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:49.387
all of the people on the cap table donated their shares to Bootstrap,

00:09:49.387 --> 00:09:52.950
because they had effectively already been paid out by mining rewards,

00:09:52.950 --> 00:09:57.205
or founders' rewards, years back. So they all donated their shares,

00:09:57.205 --> 00:10:03.280
and so then ECC was under Bootstrap, and ECC was effectively a not-for-profit with a

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:09.520
guaranteed funding stream. So, you know, Bootstrap's address and the Zcash Foundation's

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:12.089
address were actually baked into the chain.

00:10:12.089 --> 00:10:14.292
So we were guaranteed to receive...

00:10:14.292 --> 00:10:14.792
Insane.

00:10:14.792 --> 00:10:19.120
Yeah, insane. So as a public good, as a public good, it just doesn't work,

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.380
and it creates these crazy incentives. And we had these internal conversations,

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:26.080
like, the incentives are all broken. It was like, no, no, this is fine,

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.960
this is fine, they'll continue to fund us.

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.200
And I was like, nothing freaking works.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:31.700
Yeah.

00:10:31.700 --> 00:10:35.884
How can you expect the community... The only reason that the community is funding us

00:10:35.884 --> 00:10:38.912
is because we wrote the code that put our address into the chain.

00:10:38.912 --> 00:10:41.472
We needed to change it, we need to break up that structure.

00:10:41.472 --> 00:10:45.615
There are some other kind of structural things, where our mobile team is completely separated

00:10:45.615 --> 00:10:50.305
from our core engineering team. So you can't like have, create these internal walls.

00:10:50.305 --> 00:10:54.540
We're way too... We needed to bring them together.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:59.037
I feel like it's actually quite surprising that there was this idea of,

00:10:59.037 --> 00:11:04.098
oh yeah, it's baked in, like the revenue stream, it's fine to have it directly baked

00:11:04.098 --> 00:11:09.090
in through the chain, because it looks awfully a lot like a government,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:15.020
where no matter what you do, the taxes keep coming, the inflation keeps going.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:19.800
And I feel like that's sort of like exactly what Zcash is struggling against.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:21.452
It's, yeah, it's actually quite surprising that...

00:11:21.452 --> 00:11:23.834
Yeah, I mean, there were some people basically were thinking,

00:11:23.834 --> 00:11:27.539
well, people have the option, is to either opt out or to fork the chain.

00:11:27.539 --> 00:11:29.480
And so there was a fork.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:33.380
So I feel like the fork excuse is used too often.

00:11:33.380 --> 00:11:36.056
It's like, oh, if you disagree, you can just fork.

00:11:36.056 --> 00:11:40.356
But like, no, like you still need to be able to like kind of like counter and like fight,

00:11:40.356 --> 00:11:41.780
and like it's...

00:11:41.780 --> 00:11:43.916
Yeah, it's the final governance decision.

00:11:43.916 --> 00:11:46.652
It says, when everything else is broken, it's last...

00:11:46.652 --> 00:11:47.701
Yeah, it's last resort.

00:11:47.701 --> 00:11:51.340
Or if somebody just has a completely different vision of what they want to do.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:54.406
Like they want to add some kind of capability or something, because they think,

00:11:54.406 --> 00:11:56.536
and the rest of the community is not on board.

00:11:56.536 --> 00:12:00.178
I think there was enough kind of discord within the community and saying,

00:12:00.178 --> 00:12:04.136
like, look, like we need to get rid of this whole direct dev fund model.

00:12:04.136 --> 00:12:04.636
Yeah.

00:12:04.636 --> 00:12:07.740
And I was one of those people that were just like, the incentives are all broken,

00:12:07.740 --> 00:12:14.363
and what it does is the dev fund incentivizes us to try to continue to get the dev fund.

00:12:14.363 --> 00:12:14.863
Yes.

00:12:14.863 --> 00:12:16.709
It doesn't incentivize us to actually build better.

00:12:16.709 --> 00:12:17.209
Yes.

00:12:17.209 --> 00:12:20.041
And so we needed to shift so that we could build better.

00:12:20.041 --> 00:12:26.034
So this directly leads us then to the transition to Zodl, which then basically transitions

00:12:26.034 --> 00:12:29.941
ECC back from a non-profit now to a for-profit.

00:12:29.941 --> 00:12:34.353
What was the reasoning there? Was it again like better incentives?

00:12:34.353 --> 00:12:38.817
Yeah, it was better incentives. So we, through that, you know,

00:12:38.817 --> 00:12:42.736
when the price is in the doldrums, we were no longer...

00:12:42.736 --> 00:12:45.802
So what I did was refuse to take any further dev funding.

00:12:45.802 --> 00:12:50.067
So if you bake this in for the Foundation, or the community says we're going to do it,

00:12:50.067 --> 00:12:53.685
we're just not going to take the money. And so we need to figure out how to be self-sustainable

00:12:53.685 --> 00:12:58.413
by delivering products that people actually want to use.

00:12:58.413 --> 00:12:58.939
Yeah.

00:12:58.939 --> 00:13:04.863
And not be relying on this kind of effective taxation.

00:13:04.863 --> 00:13:11.160
And so, but by doing so, you know, paradoxically, I'm saying I'm cutting our revenue,

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:11.660
so...

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:12.160
Yes.

00:13:12.160 --> 00:13:13.864
And I don't know where our revenue is going to come from.

00:13:13.864 --> 00:13:14.364
Yes, yeah.

00:13:14.364 --> 00:13:19.607
And so it was a very risky move, and it hurt us for some time,

00:13:19.607 --> 00:13:22.718
right? So we used the funding that we had.

00:13:22.718 --> 00:13:25.166
We were about 30 to 50 percent understaffed.

00:13:25.166 --> 00:13:31.360
So we were trying to grow out of it with the release of Zashi in early 2024,

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:34.112
and we're starting to see good progress there.

00:13:34.112 --> 00:13:39.882
But I had this conversation with Balaji in Singapore late that year,

00:13:39.882 --> 00:13:47.704
late 2025. Balaji asked, he said, like, this is one of the most important projects in

00:13:47.704 --> 00:13:51.841
the world, not just for crypto, cryptocurrencies, but for mankind.

00:13:51.841 --> 00:13:56.892
Are you funded well enough? We need to get really big, fast.

00:13:56.892 --> 00:13:57.392
Yeah.

00:13:57.392 --> 00:14:00.260
So that it's too hard to kill.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:00.760
Yeah.

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:04.615
And Peter Van Valkenburgh had talked about something similar down at Devcon.

00:14:04.615 --> 00:14:09.054
Not specific to Zcash, but to Tornado, he's talking about Tornado Cash.

00:14:09.054 --> 00:14:12.224
And then if you're too small, the government will go after you,

00:14:12.224 --> 00:14:15.228
and they'll try to make an example of you, and there'll be a chilling effect.

00:14:15.228 --> 00:14:17.953
And that's exactly what happened with a lot of privacy projects.

00:14:17.953 --> 00:14:21.230
That's what we saw with THORChain, like backing out and saying,

00:14:21.230 --> 00:14:21.902
like, no way.

00:14:21.902 --> 00:14:25.255
And so we have to be too big to kill. And are you positioned,

00:14:25.255 --> 00:14:28.949
his question is, are you positioned to be too big to kill, effectively?

00:14:28.949 --> 00:14:32.439
I said no, we're building as fast as we can.

00:14:32.439 --> 00:14:32.939
Yeah.

00:14:32.939 --> 00:14:36.360
We'll get there. He's like, well, I know how to raise money,

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.793
let's raise money. And so we started down that path of saying we need to raise money.

00:14:39.793 --> 00:14:44.180
But in order to raise money, we really needed to be a for-profit entity.

00:14:44.180 --> 00:14:47.822
And a16z had put out a paper, talked about how foundations are dead.

00:14:47.822 --> 00:14:53.015
Some people disagree or agree with that, but I believe in market alignment and market incentives.

00:14:53.015 --> 00:15:00.407
And so ultimately getting now Zodl to a place where it's up to the market as to whether

00:15:00.407 --> 00:15:03.540
or not we succeed or fail, I think it's much healthier.

00:15:03.540 --> 00:15:06.920
This episode is sponsored by Cypherpunk.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:12.640
Cypherpunk is the first public company entirely built around privacy,

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:16.440
and they are hardcore believers in Zcash.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:20.780
And they aren't just holding Zcash, they are actively accelerating it,

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:26.024
investing in, acquiring, and building a constellation of wallets,

00:15:26.024 --> 00:15:33.008
tools, companies around ZEC that turns private money into something people actually use.

00:15:33.008 --> 00:15:39.132
If you care about where the Zcash ecosystem goes next, Cypherpunk is where it's at.

00:15:39.132 --> 00:15:42.408
Follow along at cypherpunk.com. I repeat, cypherpunk.com.

00:15:42.408 --> 00:15:44.536
Back to the video.

00:15:44.536 --> 00:15:51.130
It aligns the incentives, and I think right now Zodl is really in a position to be able

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:55.984
to finally ship. I mean, you guys raised a really great round with a lot of great investors,

00:15:55.984 --> 00:15:59.460
including people that I didn't expect would be in Zcash, like David Friedberg.

00:15:59.460 --> 00:15:59.960
Yes.

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:01.490
Very, like, oh, okay, nice.

00:16:01.490 --> 00:16:02.940
Yeah, we have him on.

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.566
Speaking of Zodl, what are your new plans then?

00:16:05.566 --> 00:16:10.144
Because I remember seeing, I think back in 2024, you had released sort of like a roadmap

00:16:10.144 --> 00:16:15.544
of stuff you had planned for Zashi back in the day, including like a vault feature and

00:16:15.544 --> 00:16:19.433
like a vault app, and, you know, there were all these things that were plans.

00:16:19.433 --> 00:16:25.804
Is that still in the works? Like what can we expect from Zodl coming up in the future?

00:16:25.804 --> 00:16:29.332
Yeah, so I'll start big, and I can go to some more granular things.

00:16:29.332 --> 00:16:32.062
Some of it we're going to talk about this week.

00:16:32.062 --> 00:16:32.636
Let's go.

00:16:32.636 --> 00:16:38.240
Yeah, and so people may be hearing some pieces of it for the first time this week as

00:16:38.240 --> 00:16:42.560
we get together. But fundamentally, I believe we're in this place...

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:47.618
We're in Prague. There was this thing that happened here called the Velvet Revolution,

00:16:47.618 --> 00:16:53.545
under communism, right, as the wall was coming down and everything was breaking apart.

00:16:53.545 --> 00:16:57.748
And the communistic system was very controlling, right?

00:16:57.748 --> 00:17:03.047
And there was a group of people, Benda, Václav Havel, some musicians,

00:17:03.047 --> 00:17:11.493
some other people, that kind of formulated this idea that we can't actually change that

00:17:11.493 --> 00:17:14.789
system from within that system, under that system's rules.

00:17:14.789 --> 00:17:15.289
Yeah.

00:17:15.289 --> 00:17:18.767
That we have to fundamentally create a parallel society.

00:17:18.767 --> 00:17:21.772
Yeah, it's not unlike Balaji's network state.

00:17:21.772 --> 00:17:27.675
Yeah. And we will have our own art, we'll have our own music.

00:17:27.675 --> 00:17:28.175
Yeah.

00:17:28.175 --> 00:17:32.812
We'll have our own writing, we'll have our own money, and we'll transact,

00:17:32.812 --> 00:17:37.368
and we'll operate as a society in parallel with the existing society.

00:17:37.368 --> 00:17:44.360
We don't have to move. And so the parallel polis, that idea was created right here.

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:51.953
And I learned about that actually in my 20s, because I would come out here during the

00:17:51.953 --> 00:17:57.089
summers and teach English, and the students would tell me about the history of the country.

00:17:57.089 --> 00:17:59.172
And it's a very beautiful idea.

00:17:59.172 --> 00:18:05.000
And likewise with our traditional financial system, I don't think you can change that

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:10.438
from within. In our current governmental systems, you can't change that from within.

00:18:10.438 --> 00:18:12.502
It's too hard, it will take a long time.

00:18:12.502 --> 00:18:16.892
Even if you could do it, it would take a long time, but there's too many entrenched powers.

00:18:16.892 --> 00:18:21.834
So what do we have to do? We have to create a parallel society,

00:18:21.834 --> 00:18:23.189
a parallel financial system.

00:18:23.189 --> 00:18:28.928
And so what we're looking at is, like, what do we need to create a parallel financial system?

00:18:28.928 --> 00:18:34.285
What are all of the pieces that need to be there, so that you can do that without trust,

00:18:34.285 --> 00:18:37.262
without having to trust an intermediary?

00:18:37.262 --> 00:18:41.940
So what does it mean, like, not to have a bank?

00:18:41.940 --> 00:18:45.300
Yeah, we have a bank today, we KYC, AML, we have all this stuff,

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:49.008
there's all these rules in place. Let's not try to be a bank.

00:18:49.008 --> 00:18:53.504
What is it that we really need, from first principles, for a new financial system?

00:18:53.504 --> 00:18:56.626
And there's a whole bunch of things that aren't there yet.

00:18:56.626 --> 00:19:02.820
So we have a need to be able to save and store our wealth, to be able to recover it,

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:08.382
or have family members recover it if we were to pass away, or if we were to kind of lose

00:19:08.382 --> 00:19:12.460
our phone or something like that. This kind of whole notion of a seed phrase,

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:16.868
that we have to either commit to memory or write down on a scrap of paper,

00:19:16.868 --> 00:19:21.360
or put it into metal and bury it in concrete in our backyard or something like that,

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:26.842
is very antiquated and not modern. And so we need those kinds of solutions.

00:19:26.842 --> 00:19:31.140
We need that full stack so that we can build this alternative financial system.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:34.684
So anywhere there's gaps, that's what we're focused on building,

00:19:34.684 --> 00:19:37.400
and removing all of the friction points.

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:40.315
Because things like a seed phrase is a friction point.

00:19:40.315 --> 00:19:42.460
I have to think about it, like, what does this mean?

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:46.084
It makes me very nervous, I'm scared, what happens if I, you know,

00:19:46.084 --> 00:19:52.369
lose my phone or something like that. So those are the problems that we need to solve.

00:19:52.369 --> 00:19:56.081
There's other things that are more mundane, like sync time.

00:19:56.081 --> 00:20:00.100
Like, I don't want to open my wallet and sit there and wait for it to sync with the chain

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:04.014
for a minute while I'm standing in line to, you know...

00:20:04.014 --> 00:20:05.437
Yeah, pay a shop.

00:20:05.437 --> 00:20:07.522
So that's something we've actually fixed.

00:20:07.522 --> 00:20:09.178
We haven't rolled out yet.

00:20:09.178 --> 00:20:10.833
Let's go.

00:20:10.833 --> 00:20:13.720
But there's things like that. That's what we're working...

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:17.659
I feel like the seed phrase thing is, it's sort of at the same time very,

00:20:17.659 --> 00:20:21.721
very powerful, because technically you could remember, like memorize your seed phrase

00:20:21.721 --> 00:20:27.100
and literally have unseizable money. Like literally no one, I mean,

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:32.200
unless we start to, we recently invent like some brain-scanning technology,

00:20:32.200 --> 00:20:34.720
you literally could not take it out of your head.

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:40.320
But at the same time, it's such a killer in terms of user experience.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:46.240
Like the vast majority of people lose that seed phrase, will store it in the cloud.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:50.490
I mean, I've been onboarding some friends to Zcash recently who are not,

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:58.682
you know, into crypto, and you actually do realize how terrible it is for them to have

00:20:58.682 --> 00:21:02.420
to figure out how to, you know, store the seed phrase and what...

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:05.700
And like, it's so... If anyone sees it, you lose your funds.

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:09.547
We really ought to get past that, especially in the day of, like,

00:21:09.547 --> 00:21:12.480
passkeys and all these new technologies that we now have.

00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:15.880
Yeah, like, I've been giving out Zcash to friends and family for many years,

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:18.440
and the majority have lost it.

00:21:18.440 --> 00:21:18.940
Yeah.

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:23.313
And that includes my kids, like paying my kids allowances or whatever in Zcash.

00:21:23.313 --> 00:21:25.028
So many wallets were lost.

00:21:25.028 --> 00:21:25.528
Yes.

00:21:25.528 --> 00:21:29.614
Like, I started saying, okay, I'm gonna hold your seed phrase for you.

00:21:29.614 --> 00:21:30.767
Yeah, which is insane.

00:21:30.767 --> 00:21:33.940
Which is insane, right? If my teenagers can't figure it out...

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:34.565
Yeah.

00:21:34.565 --> 00:21:38.940
Or it's just like there's too much friction with it, and they're just lazy.

00:21:38.940 --> 00:21:39.453
Yeah.

00:21:39.453 --> 00:21:44.324
And then it's like, oh my gosh, that like $20 worth of Zcash that you gave me is now

00:21:44.324 --> 00:21:48.045
worth four or five hundred dollars. Like, I'm trying to get it,

00:21:48.045 --> 00:21:49.620
I can't, you know.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:50.120
Yeah.

00:21:50.120 --> 00:21:52.520
That doesn't work for an alternative financial system.

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:55.887
Yeah, it's really just like recreating all the financial system in parallel,

00:21:55.887 --> 00:22:01.540
in a way. So I guess logically this would lead you to think there's a mobile wallet.

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:03.820
Eventually, I'm guessing that there will be a desktop wallet.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:08.520
I'm guessing there will probably also something being with FROST,

00:22:08.520 --> 00:22:11.380
like some multi-signature type of thing for like whales.

00:22:11.380 --> 00:22:13.860
Is that where things are going?

00:22:13.860 --> 00:22:19.353
Yeah, yeah. We already have an internal beta desktop wallet for macOS that's already

00:22:19.353 --> 00:22:24.180
built, just not released. In fact, I think it just got approved by the App Store.

00:22:24.180 --> 00:22:24.680
Nice.

00:22:24.680 --> 00:22:29.191
Maybe within the last 24 hours. So we'll have those kinds of capabilities,

00:22:29.191 --> 00:22:32.959
and then we'll be able to add kind of additional richness on top of the desktop product.

00:22:32.959 --> 00:22:36.966
There's some challenges with desktop. It's typically less secure than mobile.

00:22:36.966 --> 00:22:37.466
Yeah.

00:22:37.466 --> 00:22:40.974
There's questions about key storage, you know, what can be captured.

00:22:40.974 --> 00:22:45.214
One of the things that I love about this team is we're so close to the protocol,

00:22:45.214 --> 00:22:48.166
and like we've been working on security things for such a long time.

00:22:48.166 --> 00:22:51.910
The way we think about edge cases, the way that the engineering team thinks about edge

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:53.298
cases, like, absolutely world-class, amazing.

00:22:53.298 --> 00:23:00.098
And so you say, okay, we can, like, somebody can go and they can vibe code up a wallet

00:23:00.098 --> 00:23:03.247
or stuff like that, and then there's decisions they make about,

00:23:03.247 --> 00:23:07.081
like, how the key material is stored. What if they're rotating t-addresses?

00:23:07.081 --> 00:23:09.331
Like, is there linkability between those t-addresses?

00:23:09.331 --> 00:23:11.216
And they don't necessarily think about those things.

00:23:11.216 --> 00:23:14.612
And so it takes us a little bit longer, I think, in some cases,

00:23:14.612 --> 00:23:18.386
for some of the engineering, in order to ensure that we have it right.

00:23:18.386 --> 00:23:20.602
And then there's always trade-offs with everything.

00:23:20.602 --> 00:23:21.102
Yeah.

00:23:21.102 --> 00:23:25.050
But that we're thinking about the trade-offs well, that the user is well informed,

00:23:25.050 --> 00:23:28.316
they're not making an assumption about privacy when it's actually not true,

00:23:28.316 --> 00:23:32.672
when it can expose them. And so then we take that, we put that in the right form factor

00:23:32.672 --> 00:23:35.032
where people want to use, like a desktop or mobile or whatever,

00:23:35.032 --> 00:23:41.440
hardware devices. There's questions about infrastructure and who's running the infrastructure.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.269
What can the people that are running the infrastructure, like client services in the

00:23:45.269 --> 00:23:48.103
future, if there's PIR, private information retrieval services,

00:23:48.103 --> 00:23:51.167
if there's oblivious syncing services with Tachyon coming, like,

00:23:51.167 --> 00:23:57.166
what does that all mean in terms of who's running those services and what can be seen?

00:23:57.166 --> 00:24:00.577
And then there's the features that people need.

00:24:00.577 --> 00:24:04.992
So some of those things are cryptographic primitives, when we have things like multisig

00:24:04.992 --> 00:24:09.676
or threshold sigs. But it also might include things like time locks.

00:24:09.676 --> 00:24:15.035
Or if you think about, like, how you interact with a traditional bank account or something

00:24:15.035 --> 00:24:19.242
like that, you may limit the amount that can be transferred at any given time.

00:24:19.242 --> 00:24:24.195
So maybe you have a limit today on your wire transfers, or you have a limit on,

00:24:24.195 --> 00:24:28.988
you know, how much money can be moved over ACH, for, you know,

00:24:28.988 --> 00:24:30.094
per 24 hours.

00:24:30.094 --> 00:24:34.419
Well, users can kind of set that up themselves as well, in a decentralized fashion,

00:24:34.419 --> 00:24:39.169
if we build the right primitive, such that I can say, okay, I'm going to have this wallet,

00:24:39.169 --> 00:24:42.969
and I'm not comfortable moving more than X amount of Zcash out per day.

00:24:42.969 --> 00:24:48.187
And so I'm going to kind of lock that in, and then I can have these kind of,

00:24:48.187 --> 00:24:49.804
you know, safeguards in place.

00:24:49.804 --> 00:24:53.634
Yeah. How do you view, like, the evolution of Bitcoin compared to Zcash right now,

00:24:53.634 --> 00:24:56.340
and in the past, I guess, few months and years?

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:58.712
Yeah, both of those things are true.

00:24:58.712 --> 00:24:59.389
Let's go.

00:24:59.389 --> 00:25:05.629
Bitcoin has failed and has been captured, and Zcash is thriving.

00:25:05.629 --> 00:25:11.512
No, Bitcoin is just not usable as digital electronic cash.

00:25:11.512 --> 00:25:16.160
Like, it's not. So you can just say, like, okay, this evolved into something else,

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:20.410
and now it's like digital gold for TradFi, and that's okay.

00:25:20.410 --> 00:25:24.598
I don't think that's okay. I don't think that's what the world needs.

00:25:24.598 --> 00:25:28.799
It's fine, maybe the world wants that. That's not what I'm interested in.

00:25:28.799 --> 00:25:35.550
But what you saw happen with Bitcoin is they tried to change the system from within the

00:25:35.550 --> 00:25:38.740
system, and the system is too powerful, and it captured it.

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:44.300
So you have the BlackRocks of the world, you have the Michael Saylors of the world,

00:25:44.300 --> 00:25:49.095
financializing this asset within, and they're saying, like, we need these controls in

00:25:49.095 --> 00:25:55.077
order to manage the system, so please, government, step in, then provide us the right

00:25:55.077 --> 00:25:59.060
governance and regulations and things like that.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:05.120
And government says, oh, sure, like, we're totally happy to do this and put you in this

00:26:05.120 --> 00:26:07.700
little box. And that is what's happened with Bitcoin.

00:26:07.700 --> 00:26:11.380
And so I think it's sad. I don't think it can be saved.

00:26:11.380 --> 00:26:17.795
I think it's too far gone. It is what it is at this point, which is a regulated digital

00:26:17.795 --> 00:26:20.860
financial asset within the traditional system.

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:26.819
But I think we saw a lot of Bitcoiners, or I have personally seen a lot of Bitcoiners,

00:26:26.819 --> 00:26:32.778
who started to question whether or not this is the thing that they signed up for or got

00:26:32.778 --> 00:26:37.984
excited about. So they've either exited the system, or in a number of cases they've hedged,

00:26:37.984 --> 00:26:43.776
and they said, OK, I still believe in Bitcoin, but I'm going to take 10 percent of my

00:26:43.776 --> 00:26:48.800
holdings and I'm going to put it in Zcash as a hedge against Bitcoin.

00:26:48.800 --> 00:26:55.620
And some have completely flipped and are now Zcashers who have left Bitcoin.

00:26:55.620 --> 00:27:01.240
I fully agree. How do you prevent that capture from those actors happening?

00:27:01.240 --> 00:27:04.300
The purpose of a decentralized financial system is that it's decentralized,

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:11.722
so no one can block someone like Saylor or BlackRock or anyone else of starting those

00:27:11.722 --> 00:27:18.460
either DATs or whatever entities that are going to do that capture of the network.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:21.800
How do we ensure that that doesn't happen?

00:27:21.800 --> 00:27:25.920
We have to keep building and not ossifying.

00:27:25.920 --> 00:27:31.460
There is engagement with regulators and government officials,

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:35.700
and a recognition that doors may be open for a little while.

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:40.580
A regime change happens within a given regulatory entity, those doors close.

00:27:40.580 --> 00:27:45.711
They may open back up. But if you kowtow to those interests,

00:27:45.711 --> 00:27:48.537
you will eventually lose. You will lose.

00:27:48.537 --> 00:27:51.728
So we can't... We have to stay focused on this.

00:27:51.728 --> 00:27:56.022
We have to stay focused on this new system, allow people to opt in.

00:27:56.022 --> 00:27:59.993
If people opt in, we'll be successful. If we're not delivering something that people

00:27:59.993 --> 00:28:02.727
actually want, because they want to be under government control,

00:28:02.727 --> 00:28:07.067
then it's just going to be a little tribe over here on the side that's continuing to work.

00:28:07.067 --> 00:28:12.651
And I think that tribe will always continue to exist, but we have to make this more mainstream,

00:28:12.651 --> 00:28:16.097
too big to kill, without kind of focusing on it.

00:28:16.097 --> 00:28:19.683
Like, the whole thing with Bitcoin for a long time was like,

00:28:19.683 --> 00:28:21.791
institutions are coming, institutions are coming.

00:28:21.791 --> 00:28:25.829
Everybody got excited. Like, Bitcoin's now on the CME, right?

00:28:25.829 --> 00:28:30.103
And so, yay, this is going to make the price of the coin go up.

00:28:30.103 --> 00:28:30.603
Yeah.

00:28:30.603 --> 00:28:33.428
And you want the price of the coin to go up, and that's very reflexive.

00:28:33.428 --> 00:28:37.850
Everything, from the size of the shielded pool to the price of the coin to market interest,

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:42.638
that all stuff is very reflexive. But if you focus on just the price,

00:28:42.638 --> 00:28:47.430
and that's the only attribute of the thing, then ultimately you're going to be captured.

00:28:47.430 --> 00:28:52.576
I feel like in a lot of ways we're sort of, like, at least right now,

00:28:52.576 --> 00:28:57.502
protected against that with Zcash, given there's also this huge focus on increasing the

00:28:57.502 --> 00:29:02.605
shielded pool, which I just don't see, like, a BlackRock or any,

00:29:02.605 --> 00:29:06.587
you know, any of those entities partaking in, growing the shielded pool,

00:29:06.587 --> 00:29:10.148
and, you know, going sort of, like, against the government's interests.

00:29:10.148 --> 00:29:14.578
The structure of Zcash, I feel like, prevents by default that capture,

00:29:14.578 --> 00:29:19.280
because those entities are just not going to partake in privacy.

00:29:19.280 --> 00:29:23.006
But I guess you could have said the same thing about Bitcoin in its early days as well,

00:29:23.006 --> 00:29:25.140
and they ended up partaking in it as well.

00:29:25.140 --> 00:29:27.597
Do you think that ETFs would be good for Zcash?

00:29:27.597 --> 00:29:28.901
I don't care about them.

00:29:28.901 --> 00:29:29.401
Yeah.

00:29:29.401 --> 00:29:32.433
I think there could be some value in that.

00:29:32.433 --> 00:29:36.888
They create more capital, there's more capital inflow, so it helps with the moneyness,

00:29:36.888 --> 00:29:41.117
because you don't want massive price changes, and you need liquidity for,

00:29:41.117 --> 00:29:46.360
you know, large buyers and sellers. So there could be some advantages.

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:51.026
But I think it's not what I'm interested in personally.

00:29:51.026 --> 00:29:54.658
I don't think a lot of Zcashers... It's not what they're interested in.

00:29:54.658 --> 00:29:57.751
Like, somebody can go stuff their 401(k) so they get price exposure,

00:29:57.751 --> 00:30:01.609
but it's actually, that's working within the traditional financial system,

00:30:01.609 --> 00:30:06.572
and it's not actually building anything new or novel, or ultimately anything that really

00:30:06.572 --> 00:30:10.078
kind of benefits the world. And I think it's a net negative,

00:30:10.078 --> 00:30:13.532
in that that becomes the focus. Then the price becomes the focus:

00:30:13.532 --> 00:30:17.058
is my portfolio, my retirement portfolio, increasing over time?

00:30:17.058 --> 00:30:24.024
Rather than, are we actually driving and delivering a new system that is usable for people

00:30:24.024 --> 00:30:25.000
to protect themselves?

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:30.705
Regarding Bitcoin, what do you think is the biggest lie that the Bitcoin maximalists

00:30:30.705 --> 00:30:31.520
tell themselves?

00:30:31.520 --> 00:30:34.202
Zcash doesn't matter, or Zcash is a shitcoin.

00:30:34.202 --> 00:30:34.702
Yeah.

00:30:34.702 --> 00:30:39.440
I don't know if that's the biggest lie, but that's the most irritating one,

00:30:39.440 --> 00:30:45.612
because there's a lack of intellectual honesty from many, where they see,

00:30:45.612 --> 00:30:49.996
you know, they see what is actually happening, and then they make up shit.

00:30:49.996 --> 00:30:53.592
They make up their own narrative about what they think is happening.

00:30:53.592 --> 00:30:58.059
And it's like, you know, there's these kind of ideas where we see it across society,

00:30:58.059 --> 00:31:03.080
not just within Bitcoiners, but you get into a tribe and you get so locked into a religion,

00:31:03.080 --> 00:31:08.034
and all evidence can point you to something else, and you stop thinking about first principles.

00:31:08.034 --> 00:31:12.793
You stop thinking about what's actually happening, because you're so married to this idea.

00:31:12.793 --> 00:31:18.276
So there's people, they'll go on interviews, I've seen them on CoinDesk or other places,

00:31:18.276 --> 00:31:21.418
and they make these outrageous statements that are just not...

00:31:21.418 --> 00:31:22.684
And they're smart people.

00:31:22.684 --> 00:31:23.184
Yeah.

00:31:23.184 --> 00:31:28.520
Not rooted in any truth. It's just like they're so blind to what the actual truth is,

00:31:28.520 --> 00:31:33.920
because they're so locked in. They just don't have the, I don't know if it's a mental

00:31:33.920 --> 00:31:37.998
clarity, or, you know, they're able to kind of change their thinking.

00:31:37.998 --> 00:31:41.351
I think because, I mean, you know, they're clearly, you know,

00:31:41.351 --> 00:31:47.120
all their bags are in Bitcoin, so I guess they're clearly incentivized to go promote it.

00:31:47.120 --> 00:31:51.600
There's the denial of the importance of privacy, which I think is,

00:31:51.600 --> 00:31:56.800
especially in this day and age with AI, how could you not recognize that privacy on Bitcoin

00:31:56.800 --> 00:32:02.939
right now is a huge issue? I think there's also quantum, which to me is just shocking,

00:32:02.939 --> 00:32:08.586
especially now with the US administration that accelerated the deadline to,

00:32:08.586 --> 00:32:12.795
I think it's 2031 now, from 2035 previously.

00:32:12.795 --> 00:32:18.236
And knowing that Bitcoin will take years to upgrade to post-quantum,

00:32:18.236 --> 00:32:23.050
once there is consensus on it, it feels irresponsible, frankly.

00:32:23.050 --> 00:32:27.857
It is irresponsible. So post-quantum is great, that's a great example.

00:32:27.857 --> 00:32:33.495
There's different opinions on where we're going to see the first kind of demonstrable

00:32:33.495 --> 00:32:34.613
quantum computer.

00:32:34.613 --> 00:32:35.172
Yeah.

00:32:35.172 --> 00:32:40.201
And those seem to be inching forward, not away.

00:32:40.201 --> 00:32:43.261
So we had like 2035, then it became like 2031.

00:32:43.261 --> 00:32:46.836
Like, I've heard new estimates of 2028 could be where we see the first...

00:32:46.836 --> 00:32:48.031
I mean, no one knows, but...

00:32:48.031 --> 00:32:50.022
Yeah, it's like, start waiting till we have Q-Day.

00:32:50.022 --> 00:32:55.376
And so you have to be prepared for that outcome, even if you don't believe that.

00:32:55.376 --> 00:32:58.187
You say, oh, it's gonna be 10 years off.

00:32:58.187 --> 00:33:01.164
I've heard the estimates for Bitcoin, like, to get a change in,

00:33:01.164 --> 00:33:04.107
to get a whatever, it's probably at least five years.

00:33:04.107 --> 00:33:08.780
If you believe that that is a possibility, to your point, it's totally irresponsible

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:14.875
to say, oh, it's like, I don't, because I don't think so, I have no knowledge,

00:33:14.875 --> 00:33:19.702
but I don't think so, then therefore we won't do anything.

00:33:19.702 --> 00:33:20.202
Ridiculous.

00:33:20.202 --> 00:33:23.296
And the privacy is the other. Yeah, it's another one, as you mentioned,

00:33:23.296 --> 00:33:28.347
where, and that's been out there for a long time, and it kind of varies from,

00:33:28.347 --> 00:33:31.815
well, it's verifiability that is like the key attribute.

00:33:31.815 --> 00:33:35.342
And that's the key attribute because it's the key attribute for number go up.

00:33:35.342 --> 00:33:38.954
And so then that becomes more important than privacy.

00:33:38.954 --> 00:33:44.880
Privacy is absolutely, absolutely necessary for a crypto that can't be captured,

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:46.700
that can't be censored. Then that disregard...

00:33:46.700 --> 00:33:50.698
And then there's others that will say, okay, we could have privacy if we want it,

00:33:50.698 --> 00:33:53.620
we're just gonna, like, add a sidechain, we'll add it later.

00:33:53.620 --> 00:33:55.780
That was the original thing with Zcash, right?

00:33:55.780 --> 00:34:01.502
Is the Bitcoin Core devs said, well, we'll add it later, if Zcash technology starts to

00:34:01.502 --> 00:34:06.423
look good and usable. And it did, but they're like, no, no, no,

00:34:06.423 --> 00:34:09.956
we're too busy trying to work within the traditional financial system,

00:34:09.956 --> 00:34:11.973
and they don't want that. So...

00:34:11.973 --> 00:34:15.273
Yeah. This episode is brought to you by Cypherpunk.

00:34:15.273 --> 00:34:22.107
Surveillance is now one API call away, and your data has never been more valuable.

00:34:22.107 --> 00:34:29.030
Cypherpunk is the public company backing Zcash and building up the Zcash ecosystem to

00:34:29.030 --> 00:34:33.473
make financial privacy default. Learn more about Cypherpunk at cypherpunk.com.

00:34:33.473 --> 00:34:35.144
Back to the episode.

00:34:35.144 --> 00:34:41.375
I've seen, like, a tweet from Peter Todd, who is a real smart guy.

00:34:41.375 --> 00:34:46.665
Like, he participated in the, I'm understanding he's participated in the Zcash ceremony,

00:34:46.665 --> 00:34:50.515
brilliant developer, I'm sure, who basically was stating, oh,

00:34:50.515 --> 00:34:55.236
because of the, you know, I think it was reacting to the soundness bug that happened,

00:34:55.236 --> 00:35:00.268
like, you know, a few weeks ago. It was reacting, well, this is why Bitcoin will never

00:35:00.268 --> 00:35:03.260
have privacy, you know, there are all these risks.

00:35:03.260 --> 00:35:08.460
And it's like, this was like a patchable bug, it was patched.

00:35:08.460 --> 00:35:15.100
So what he's saying is, okay, so we're going to let Bitcoin become a surveillance chain,

00:35:15.100 --> 00:35:18.880
because this is literally what it will lead to with advanced AI.

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:23.040
Once you can, I mean, you can already, arguably, with Chainalysis and other companies,

00:35:23.040 --> 00:35:28.035
track all the transactions. But going to let it become a surveillance chain all because

00:35:28.035 --> 00:35:33.760
of one or two patchable bugs that could have been, you know,

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:37.300
could have been found in production. I feel like that's just,

00:35:37.300 --> 00:35:39.500
again, it's irresponsible, it feels like.

00:35:39.500 --> 00:35:43.040
Well, let's be clear. I mean, Bitcoin had inflation bugs.

00:35:43.040 --> 00:35:43.980
Yes.

00:35:43.980 --> 00:35:45.860
So it's ridiculous.

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:46.480
Exactly.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:51.280
And then took action to be able to kind of address those things.

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:54.366
But it depends on what you're optimizing for and what your incentives are,

00:35:54.366 --> 00:35:56.740
which goes back to kind of this conversation about incentives.

00:35:56.740 --> 00:36:00.730
If you're maximizing or optimizing for incentives for price,

00:36:00.730 --> 00:36:07.660
if that's the only thing that matters, that's kind of what his argument rests on.

00:36:07.660 --> 00:36:12.132
If you're optimizing for usable money that protects people's security and safety and

00:36:12.132 --> 00:36:16.620
all of those things, then you have to optimize for something else.

00:36:16.620 --> 00:36:17.940
And there's trade-offs with all those things.

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:22.700
Now, we can get better and better with soundness and ensuring that we have it.

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:25.640
And that work is happening now. It's happening with Ironwood.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:28.520
It'll happen further with formal verification and things that we're doing.

00:36:28.520 --> 00:36:33.620
But this should be the goal, is to optimize for this thing and not optimize for just

00:36:33.620 --> 00:36:34.820
the price going up.

00:36:34.820 --> 00:36:40.160
A lot of people have positioned Zcash sort of like as an insurance for Bitcoin.

00:36:40.160 --> 00:36:46.018
Do you think Zcash will at some point overtake Bitcoin, or do you think it will stay

00:36:46.018 --> 00:36:49.600
sort of like the silver to Bitcoin's gold, essentially?

00:36:49.600 --> 00:36:51.422
Yeah, it's not the silver to Bitcoin's gold.

00:36:51.422 --> 00:36:55.017
It's the alternative to Bitcoin. So it just ultimately depends on what people want in

00:36:55.017 --> 00:36:58.899
the world. If they want to live within the traditional financial system,

00:36:58.899 --> 00:37:03.073
under the constraints of, you know, these world leaders, these central banks,

00:37:03.073 --> 00:37:08.338
then they will opt into Bitcoin. If they want an alternative where they're free,

00:37:08.338 --> 00:37:15.480
then they will opt into Zcash as an alternative to that, this kind of new financial system.

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:19.220
Ultimately, I think people want to be free.

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:22.060
So that answers your question.

00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:25.420
What does success look like for Zcash in 5–10 years?

00:37:25.420 --> 00:37:27.340
Zcash in the hands of billions of people.

00:37:27.340 --> 00:37:30.460
That's the goal. If it's just in the hands of 30, 50 people,

00:37:30.460 --> 00:37:36.649
like, it doesn't matter. It's fine for purpose that way, but yeah,

00:37:36.649 --> 00:37:38.562
the goal is mass adoption.

00:37:38.562 --> 00:37:43.013
Do you think more of Zcash as a store of value or as a currency?

00:37:43.013 --> 00:37:47.727
I don't care about that either. Like, I know people like to have this debate.

00:37:47.727 --> 00:37:48.227
Yeah.

00:37:48.227 --> 00:37:52.439
And create this dichotomy. But, like, even thinking about it in terms of the traditional

00:37:52.439 --> 00:37:55.791
sense, is the US dollar a store of value, or is it a medium of exchange,

00:37:55.791 --> 00:38:00.329
or is it a unit of account? It's all of those things, right?

00:38:00.329 --> 00:38:04.700
I've got some savings, I want to put it into a bank account,

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:10.407
I want to earn some yield on it. Maybe it's not that much yield compared to other securities

00:38:10.407 --> 00:38:14.948
or other assets that are higher risk, but it's a lower-risk store of value,

00:38:14.948 --> 00:38:19.260
because I'm betting on the US, you know, the US dollar, to maintain some stable rate.

00:38:19.260 --> 00:38:23.081
Plus I get my, you know, maybe three and a half percent on a high-yield account.

00:38:23.081 --> 00:38:27.580
But then, so I store it in, like, a battery until I want to move it,

00:38:27.580 --> 00:38:32.410
until I want to go buy a car, or pay for my kids' college or something like that.

00:38:32.410 --> 00:38:36.103
And then I remove it as a store of value and it becomes a medium of exchange.

00:38:36.103 --> 00:38:42.000
It changes the dynamics of it. So I see Zcash in the same way.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:43.920
I don't see it any different.

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:47.418
Regarding the shielded transactions, I believe you did a podcast,

00:38:47.418 --> 00:38:52.186
I think it was a year or two ago, with Origin, as well as Zooko was there,

00:38:52.186 --> 00:38:56.320
I think, where I think Zooko was thinking sort of like the other side,

00:38:56.320 --> 00:39:00.927
and you were sort of like defending the position that we should basically just remove

00:39:00.927 --> 00:39:07.316
the transparent pool, or, like, you know, long-term downsize it and eventually kill it

00:39:07.316 --> 00:39:11.420
in favor of just shielded only. Is that something you still believe?

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:14.984
The primary argument for the transparent pool would be, well,

00:39:14.984 --> 00:39:19.700
it helps with exchanges. It helps, I guess, reassure a bit the regulators.

00:39:19.700 --> 00:39:23.980
It can help with compliance, although we do have, like, viewing keys and stuff.

00:39:23.980 --> 00:39:28.163
Yeah, I mean, list who you mentioned: exchanges, which are now banks,

00:39:28.163 --> 00:39:32.695
regulated banks, and regulators. And so again, like, who are you optimizing for?

00:39:32.695 --> 00:39:38.273
Yeah, in order for this to work at mass scale, in order to protect everybody's security...

00:39:38.273 --> 00:39:41.759
Anytime you do any kind of transaction, there's leaky information,

00:39:41.759 --> 00:39:46.291
right? There always is. When you move money, there's always something that leaks.

00:39:46.291 --> 00:39:49.428
Doesn't matter how private, even a US dollar, right?

00:39:49.428 --> 00:39:55.925
I hand you a $20 bill, maybe there's a camera in the room, all this kind of stuff.

00:39:55.925 --> 00:39:58.357
But you want to remove as much leakage as possible.

00:39:58.357 --> 00:40:00.850
The more you have transparent addresses, the more information leaks.

00:40:00.850 --> 00:40:05.391
So how do we remove all of those leakages, or as much of those leakages as we can,

00:40:05.391 --> 00:40:07.420
so that people don't have the mental load?

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:12.420
And that's like, if you think, okay, I want to spend this, but am I spending it privately?

00:40:12.420 --> 00:40:14.913
What am I revealing? Who am I revealing to the world?

00:40:14.913 --> 00:40:15.413
Yeah.

00:40:15.413 --> 00:40:17.872
And you've got to do that on every transaction, right?

00:40:17.872 --> 00:40:21.676
The mental load associated with that, like, it just doesn't work.

00:40:21.676 --> 00:40:24.779
So, like, remove that friction of what has to go on.

00:40:24.779 --> 00:40:26.989
And people don't even understand, right?

00:40:26.989 --> 00:40:28.916
They don't understand, in many cases.

00:40:28.916 --> 00:40:34.073
Like, I had a Zcasher who was a whale that I was talking to,

00:40:34.073 --> 00:40:37.627
and I was asking him what wallet he was on.

00:40:37.627 --> 00:40:44.087
I won't name the wallet, but he was using a wallet, and all of his Zcash was transparent.

00:40:44.087 --> 00:40:47.632
But he thought it was all shielded. He's like, I'm in Zcash for the privacy.

00:40:47.632 --> 00:40:53.840
I was like, what wallet are you using? Do you realize that none of your Zcash is private?

00:40:53.840 --> 00:40:54.960
And he's like, what?

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:55.460
Yeah.

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:57.360
Because the brand is privacy.

00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.160
Yeah.

00:40:58.160 --> 00:41:03.209
But he didn't understand, even though he had made significant investment into it...

00:41:03.209 --> 00:41:03.709
Yeah.

00:41:03.709 --> 00:41:05.021
That he was transparent.

00:41:05.021 --> 00:41:05.521
Yeah.

00:41:05.521 --> 00:41:09.732
What was leaking. So we have to close those leakages, remove that mental friction,

00:41:09.732 --> 00:41:12.320
so that we don't have to do it through education.

00:41:12.320 --> 00:41:15.302
If we have to do it through education, we've freaking failed.

00:41:15.302 --> 00:41:15.802
Yeah.

00:41:15.802 --> 00:41:19.147
It just has to work, and it has to be usable.

00:41:19.147 --> 00:41:25.380
So I think t-addresses, ultimately, they were a nice tool for onboarding the world,

00:41:25.380 --> 00:41:28.955
with, like, exchanges and stuff, because, like, Sprout, again,

00:41:28.955 --> 00:41:33.333
an exchange couldn't support shielded transactions out of the gate.

00:41:33.333 --> 00:41:39.125
But even those exchanges today, there is no reason, there is no compliance reason that

00:41:39.125 --> 00:41:43.876
they can't support the transactions. So even if you buy into that system,

00:41:43.876 --> 00:41:48.815
which I don't, and you buy into the KYC system, which I don't,

00:41:48.815 --> 00:41:50.353
they KYC all their customers.

00:41:50.353 --> 00:41:50.853
Yeah.

00:41:50.853 --> 00:41:52.574
So they know who the customers are.

00:41:52.574 --> 00:41:53.074
Yeah.

00:41:53.074 --> 00:41:56.668
And right now, most exchanges in the world, with the exception of Binance,

00:41:56.668 --> 00:42:02.080
you can send from a shielded address to a transparent address on the exchange,

00:42:02.080 --> 00:42:04.240
and the exchange can't see the source of funds.

00:42:04.240 --> 00:42:10.278
You have to ask. They do due diligence, and they say, this huge chunk of money came in,

00:42:10.278 --> 00:42:15.456
where did it come from? So they follow the due diligence process under their regulatory regime.

00:42:15.456 --> 00:42:20.200
Likewise, if you send out from an exchange, there's no reason,

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:25.219
there's no regulatory reason that they can't send to a shielded address.

00:42:25.219 --> 00:42:26.193
It's all technical.

00:42:26.193 --> 00:42:26.693
Yeah.

00:42:26.693 --> 00:42:31.610
So there's one exchange, Gemini, that made the decision that we're going to support shielded addresses.

00:42:31.610 --> 00:42:34.109
It took engineering work. They had to make an investment.

00:42:34.109 --> 00:42:37.760
They had to modify some curves on their HSMs in order to make this stuff work.

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:42.846
But they did it for the benefit of the users, and it actually provides more security

00:42:42.846 --> 00:42:46.445
for the users than any other exchange. Because any other exchange,

00:42:46.445 --> 00:42:48.428
you're sending from Binance, for example...

00:42:48.428 --> 00:42:48.928
Yeah.

00:42:48.928 --> 00:42:53.843
To a transparent address, it's leaking all kinds of information.

00:42:53.843 --> 00:42:59.865
Gemini can still comply under NYDFS, the most restrictive financial regulator in the world.

00:42:59.865 --> 00:43:00.365
Yeah.

00:43:00.365 --> 00:43:08.420
Can still deploy, you know, can use due diligence appropriately.

00:43:08.420 --> 00:43:11.680
And so the reasons behind it... Like, I worked with Coinbase,

00:43:11.680 --> 00:43:18.860
and Coinbase was great, and it was great for accessibility, so more people could get access.

00:43:18.860 --> 00:43:23.260
There were no DEXs or anything available for Zcash at the time.

00:43:23.260 --> 00:43:30.144
And they weren't going to support shielded addresses, but they decided just to allow

00:43:30.144 --> 00:43:32.646
for the one way and not the other way. Why?

00:43:32.646 --> 00:43:36.643
They said, because until the market tells us that we need to support it,

00:43:36.643 --> 00:43:40.641
we're not going to invest the engineering resources, because we're going to invest engineering

00:43:40.641 --> 00:43:44.639
resources in the coins, adding more coins that the rest of the world wants,

00:43:44.639 --> 00:43:47.980
in order to increase their revenue, and not make the investment.

00:43:47.980 --> 00:43:51.860
So I think as Zcash gets bigger, more people start to demand it.

00:43:51.860 --> 00:43:55.857
Even those regulated entities, who can still comply with the regulators,

00:43:55.857 --> 00:43:58.400
will be able to support shielded Zcash.

00:43:58.400 --> 00:44:04.837
I think one of the core arguments, on top of what you said about all the friction it

00:44:04.837 --> 00:44:09.128
causes and just working within the constraints of the traditional financial system,

00:44:09.128 --> 00:44:14.134
is also quantum, which is that the shielded pool is just objectively more secure,

00:44:14.134 --> 00:44:20.607
given public keys are not exposed. And so if only for that, it would make sense to transition

00:44:20.607 --> 00:44:24.260
fully to a shielded pool eventually. This leads me to, like,

00:44:24.260 --> 00:44:27.940
quantum recoverability, which should be added to Zcash soon.

00:44:27.940 --> 00:44:32.560
Is that something that will be available, like, in this Zodl app?

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:38.920
Like, how will that UX work of recovering your funds in case a quantum attack happens?

00:44:38.920 --> 00:44:42.734
Yeah, well, first, the pool itself, the Ironwood pool itself,

00:44:42.734 --> 00:44:49.027
will be quantum recoverable. So in the case, in the advent that a quantum adversary shows

00:44:49.027 --> 00:44:54.588
up, Q-Day happens, then funds can easily move to another pool without exposing users

00:44:54.588 --> 00:44:58.880
to that risk. But a lot more work needs to happen.

00:44:58.880 --> 00:45:05.480
There's still elliptic curve cryptography, which is susceptible to quantum attack,

00:45:05.480 --> 00:45:09.320
in Zcash. And so that still needs to be resolved.

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:11.900
But it puts us in a place where we can move quickly.

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:14.260
We know how to make it post-quantum.

00:45:14.260 --> 00:45:20.000
There is conversations that we'll have this week with all the developers here in Prague,

00:45:20.000 --> 00:45:24.940
where we'll talk about what that roadmap looks like, how that intersects with Tachyon.

00:45:24.940 --> 00:45:28.480
There's some decisions and prioritization that needs to be made.

00:45:28.480 --> 00:45:34.874
But I think we're in the position that if we want it, we can have it within the next

00:45:34.874 --> 00:45:40.040
one to two years. It just depends on how we want to prioritize things and accelerate things.

00:45:40.040 --> 00:45:44.260
So there's a clear roadmap there. We know how to do it.

00:45:44.260 --> 00:45:47.100
It will be in there as soon as it's needed.

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:49.380
And in the interim, it's quantum recoverable.

00:45:49.380 --> 00:45:52.360
What the user experience looks like, I think, is still to be determined,

00:45:52.360 --> 00:45:58.140
and will be determined based on where things kind of roll out on the roadmap.

00:45:58.140 --> 00:46:01.400
Do you think it's fair to say that Zcash is, or is becoming at least,

00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:06.980
the safest, most secure place to store your funds right now in crypto?

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:10.100
Yes, full stop. We're leading the way with quantum.

00:46:10.100 --> 00:46:14.100
Now with formal verification, it's getting even better.

00:46:14.100 --> 00:46:19.120
Privacy, generally speaking, is already also just a security benefit in and of itself.

00:46:19.120 --> 00:46:25.232
What is your timeline for when I can literally just tap and pay with,

00:46:25.232 --> 00:46:31.320
like, maybe a Zodl card or something at a store, and, like, just instant payments?

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:34.060
Right now, yeah, you could technically pay.

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:36.280
I've never actually tried the Flexa integration.

00:46:36.280 --> 00:46:41.740
I really should. I don't know how quick it would be, but with the block times and stuff,

00:46:41.740 --> 00:46:46.633
and with all that, I'm guessing it would still take, like, some time for the transaction

00:46:46.633 --> 00:46:50.200
to go through. So the UX is probably not there yet, I'm assuming.

00:46:50.200 --> 00:46:52.380
Yeah, what is your timeline for that?

00:46:52.380 --> 00:46:55.660
Yeah, as soon as possible. Again, we'll talk about that a little bit this week.

00:46:55.660 --> 00:47:01.000
We need to do some R&D. Part of that is with a vendor.

00:47:01.000 --> 00:47:05.580
One of the nice things with a vendor, they need to accept whatever they're going to accept.

00:47:05.580 --> 00:47:11.640
So the timeline for which each vendor around the world accepts shielded ZEC,

00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:16.580
that's a much longer timeline. But we don't have to wait for that.

00:47:16.580 --> 00:47:21.970
So one of the things that Flexa did, which is great, is they integrate with legacy point-of-sale

00:47:21.970 --> 00:47:27.500
systems, so that the stores don't have to change too much in terms of their internal technology.

00:47:27.500 --> 00:47:33.456
So I can go to Chipotle or Barnes & Noble or Ulta Beauty or wherever,

00:47:33.456 --> 00:47:38.300
and I can pay, and it's instant, or very near instant, right?

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:42.860
So you've got a line of people behind you, you know, you don't want to sit there forever

00:47:42.860 --> 00:47:48.344
waiting for a confirmation to confirm. And so what happens is then tokens are collateralized

00:47:48.344 --> 00:47:53.180
on the back end in order to allow that transaction to be effected,

00:47:53.180 --> 00:47:59.960
even if the confirmation time for Zcash is slower than that particular instant.

00:47:59.960 --> 00:48:04.745
And likewise, like what we do with swaps, we have something called CrossPay within Zodl

00:48:04.745 --> 00:48:09.442
that allows you to pay, like in USDC, for example, or Tether,

00:48:09.442 --> 00:48:14.945
whatever, maybe whatever new stablecoin this whole group is coming up with,

00:48:14.945 --> 00:48:17.716
whatever coin that the vendor is comfortable with.

00:48:17.716 --> 00:48:23.117
We can pay, and we can pay near instantly with CrossPay.

00:48:23.117 --> 00:48:26.094
Currently, that's leveraging NEAR Intents.

00:48:26.094 --> 00:48:29.980
And again, there's market makers that are effectively collateralizing.

00:48:29.980 --> 00:48:35.820
So we can kind of speed up that whole process and allow those transactions to be effected quickly.

00:48:35.820 --> 00:48:40.876
We want to minimize that. It needs to be as fast and as scalable as the traditional financial

00:48:40.876 --> 00:48:43.640
system, as the Visas, Mastercards of the world.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:49.720
So we've got work to do to close that gap, but I think we have a roadmap to do that.

00:48:49.720 --> 00:48:56.579
As soon as they adopt stablecoins, because with CrossPay you can effectively just convert

00:48:56.579 --> 00:49:00.567
any Zcash to any other currency that they accept.

00:49:00.567 --> 00:49:04.537
So whatever crypto that they accept, as long as they accept one,

00:49:04.537 --> 00:49:08.217
Zcash will just work with it, which is the power of CrossPay.

00:49:08.217 --> 00:49:11.589
Yeah, and it's kind of the Trojan horse, in that we can say,

00:49:11.589 --> 00:49:14.700
okay, we're not trying to conform to the traditional financial system.

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:19.615
Yeah, and like, because we got asked, for example, we got asked to help create a CBDC,

00:49:19.615 --> 00:49:27.775
and we said no. But all of these other groups do, because they see stablecoins are working.

00:49:27.775 --> 00:49:28.275
Yeah.

00:49:28.275 --> 00:49:31.205
And people are buying into them. They're effectively a CBDC,

00:49:31.205 --> 00:49:34.052
they're just an outsourced CBDC. It's horrible.

00:49:34.052 --> 00:49:37.493
But if somebody wants to accept them, then fine.

00:49:37.493 --> 00:49:40.040
You can stay private, and we're gonna follow.

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:43.456
They're gonna create this massive wave, and we're just gonna surf it.

00:49:43.456 --> 00:49:43.956
Yeah.

00:49:43.956 --> 00:49:48.471
And then people can pay in whatever that vendor wants to take,

00:49:48.471 --> 00:49:51.600
and stay safe and secure and private.

00:49:51.600 --> 00:49:56.262
Yeah, perfect. Well, thanks, Josh, and yeah, let's do this again soon.

00:49:56.262 --> 00:49:57.403
All right, awesome. Thank you.

00:49:57.403 --> 00:49:59.632
Thank you.
